\begin{lemma} \label{lemma:hermitian_modules_over_product_of_involution_rings_split_along_involution_ring_factors}
    Let $R$ be a ring with involution. Write $R \cong \prod_i R_i$
    where the product is a finite product of involution rings, and the isomorphism is of involution rings. 
    
    Hermitian modules over $R$ correspond to tuples of Hermitian modules over $R_i$. More precisely, letting $V$ be any $R$-module, writing $V_i$ for the $R_i$-component of $V$, letting $\varepsilon_i \in R_i$ satisfy $\varepsilon_i \overline{\varepsilon_i} = 1$, and letting $\epsilon$ be the element of $R$ whose $R_i$-component is $\varepsilon_i$,  there is a bijection
        \begin{align*}
        \left\{ \begin{array}{l} \text{Tuples } (H_i)_i \text{ where } \\ H_i: V_i \times V_i \to R_i \\ \text{are } \varepsilon_i\text{-Hermitian forms} \end{array} \right\} &\to \left\{ \begin{array}{l} \varepsilon\text{-Hermitian forms } \\ H: V \times V \to R \end{array}\right\}  \\
        (H_i)_i &\mapsto \perp_i H_i.
        \end{align*}
    To discuss $\perp_i H_i$, note that we regard $H_i: V_i \times V_i \to R_i$ as a $\varepsilon$-Hermitian form over $R$ via the injection $R_i \hookrightarrow \prod_i R_i \cong R$ sending an element $d_i \in R_i$ to the element of $\prod_i R_i$ whose $i$th component is $d_i$ and whose other components are $0$. 
    
    Furthermore, given that each $V_i$ is a finitely generated and projective $R_i$-module (equivalently $V$ is a finitely generated and projective $R$-module), the $H_i$ are all nonsingular if and only if $\perp_i H_i$ is nonsingular.
\end{lemma}